 ## Background
-The Image-based Rendering Cost Model was introduced in the context of comparing remote visualization strategies for the interactive exploration of large data sets [S1]. It is part of a series of models (Model 1, Model 2, Model 3) designed to evaluate the performance of parallel visualization servers against image-based rendering techniques [S1]. The model builds on the assumption that isosurfaces are computed in parallel on a remote server, leveraging the [Ray-grid traversal](17-ray-grid-traversal.md) logic for efficient isosurface extraction, but focuses on the post-processing and transmission phases [S1].
+The Image-based Rendering Cost Model was introduced to compare remote visualization strategies for the interactive exploration of large data sets [S1]. It forms part of a series of analytical models (Models 1, 2, and 3) designed to evaluate the performance of parallel visualization servers against image-based rendering and subsampling techniques [S1]. The model was developed to quantify the trade-offs between parallel computation, network transmission, and latency in distributed visualization architectures [S1].
 
